-# PS5 Relapse Exploit
+# PS5 Relapse · pldmgr edition (start button + built-in payload manager)
 Supported firmware: 7.00 through 13.60.
 
+A modified build of the Relapse exploit page with a **WebKitty-style UI**:
+a start screen with a **Start Jailbreak button**, and a **built-in payload
+manager** listing **all 133 payloads from the
+[evoX-CoreOS](https://github.com/nexgen999/evoX-CoreOS) catalog** (pldmgr
+included) plus a Custom tab for your own `.elf` files.
+
+**Nothing is auto-sent except elfldr.** After the jailbreak the kernel loader
+starts **elfldr** (the port-9021 ps5-payload-dev ELF loader daemon) and stops
+there - every payload is one tap away in the manager, delivered on demand.
+
+## How it works
+1. **Page open (no exploit yet):** the kexp shellcode and elfldr are mirrored
+   into PS5 localStorage, the payload catalog renders, the site caches itself
+   (see below). Nothing runs until you press **Start Jailbreak** (or tap a
+   payload card, which queues it and auto-starts the chain).
+2. **Start Jailbreak:** webkit exploit → kernel exploit → **elfldr starts on
+   port 9021**. If you had tapped payloads while it ran, they are delivered
+   the moment the loader is up.
+3. **Payload manager:** every card (133 evoX payloads + your custom files) is
+   delivered through raw-socket injection (`src/inject.js`): after the kernel
+   exploit the browser process is root and sandbox-escaped, so the page does
+   exactly what the documented `nc -q0 <ps5> 9021 < payload.elf` does -
+   `socket` → `connect 127.0.0.1:9021` → stream the raw ELF → `shutdown` -
+   all through ROP syscalls, **never** through the browser network stack.
+   Catalog payloads are downloaded from evoX-CoreOS (CORS is open there),
+   sha256-verified against the catalog, and their first stdout lines are
+   mirrored into the on-screen log as proof of life.
+
+## Self-caching (works on your own host, e.g. port 12423)
+The page caches itself to the PS5 in **two modes**, picked automatically:
+
+- **https / localhost hosting** (GitHub Pages etc.): a **service worker**
+  (`sw.js`) keeps the whole site inside the PS5 webkit cache - it opens and
+  runs with zero network after the first visit.
+- **plain-http self-hosting** (e.g. `http://<pc-ip>:12423`): service workers
+  do not exist on insecure origins, so the **bootstrap in `index.html`
+  mirrors every site asset into PS5 localStorage** on the first visit and
+  serves the page **inline from PS5 storage** from then on. Only the small
+  HTML shell still comes from your server - everything else (all code,
+  elfldr, offsets, the catalog) is on the console. A quiet weekly refresh
+  re-syncs the stored copy.
+
+To self-host: `python3 serve.py` (defaults to **port 12423**, prints the URL),
+then open `http://<pc-ip>:12423/` in the PS5 browser once while your server
+runs. From the second visit the page needs no server for anything except the
+HTML shell itself.
+
+## Why pldmgr is not started by the kernel loader
+The Relapse kernel loader is minimal: it starts elfldr (3 basic SPRX deps:
+libkernel_web, libSceLibcInternal, libSceNet) fine. pldmgr needs **nine**
+SPRX (adds libSceNetCtl, libSceUserService, libSceSystemService,
+libSceAppInstUtil, libSceHttp2, libSceSsl). Loading it directly hangs in
+kernel mode - the loader thread never returns, the browser process becomes
+unkillable, and the console browser then splash-loops on every launch until
+reboot. This is the "only elfldr runs / browser won't open anymore" glitch.
+The fix: never hand pldmgr to the kernel loader; deliver it through the full
+ps5-payload-dev ELF loader instead (it resolves SPRX via
+`sys_dynlib_process_needed_and_relocate` and spawns payloads as real
+processes). If your console is already stuck in the splash loop: **reboot
+once** - nothing is written to disk, the state clears on reboot.
+
+## What changed vs upstream
+- `index.html`: WebKitty-style two-screen UI (start screen + exploit screen
+  with console and payload manager). The only script tag is the **bootstrap**,
+  which loads the site assets either from `./src/...` (secure origins, SW
+  caching) or inline from PS5 localStorage (plain-http self-hosting).
+- `src/payload_catalog.js` (new): all 133 evoX-CoreOS payloads embedded
+  (name/version/description/category/url/sha256), regenerated by
+  `scripts/build_payload_catalog.py`; `src/ui.js` refreshes the list from the
+  live evoX JSON when the network is up.
+- `src/ui.js` (new): tabs (All/Homebrew/Tools/Loaders/Servers/Debug/Custom),
+  search, category badges, custom `.elf` upload, queue-then-delivery logic.
+- `src/main.js`: **elfldr is the only auto-loaded payload**; exposes the live
+  ROP session (`window.RelapseDeliver`) so the manager can send payloads any
+  time while the page stays open. All deliveries go through one serialized
+  ROP chain.
+- `src/inject.js` (new): raw-socket ELF injection into port 9021 via ROP
+  syscalls, with partial-write handling and a non-blocking stdout drain.
+- `src/payload_cache.js` (new): cache-first resolver (PS5 localStorage →
+  embedded base64 → network) for elfldr and the kexp shellcode.
+- `src/payload_data.js`: elfldr (397 KB) + kexp shellcode embedded as base64
+  with sha256 metadata; regenerated by `scripts/build_payload_js.py`.
+- `src/kexp.js`, `src/relapse_exploit.js`, `src/webkit.js`,
+  `src/utils/mem.js`, `src/site.js`: classic scripts (no module imports) so
+  the whole site can boot from PS5 localStorage; exposed as
+  `window.RelapseKexp/RelapseKernel/RelapseWebKit/RelapseMem`.
+- `sw.js` (new): service worker for secure origins - page + offsets are
+  network-first (updates win when online), all other assets cache-first.
+  Bump `VERSION` in `sw.js` and the bootstrap `VERSION` in `index.html`
+  whenever you change site files.
+- `serve.py`: tiny static server with cache-friendly headers, defaults to
+  **port 12423**.
+
 ## Usage
-The default payloads are stored in `payloads/` after a successful run, the ELF loader listens on port `9021`.
+Open the page in the PS5 browser. Press **Start Jailbreak** (or the PS logo),
+wait for `elfldr ... is up on port 9021`, then tap any payload card. The
+on-screen log shows each stage:
+
+```
+[+] kexp v2026-05-25 shellcode mapped at 0x...
+[+] elfldr v13.60 is up on port 9021
+[+] payload manager unlocked - every payload below is one tap away
+[*] downloading Pldmgr V0.6.3 from evoX-CoreOS...
+[+] Pldmgr V0.6.3 checksum verified (2795992 bytes)
+[+] Pldmgr V0.6.3 handed to the ELF loader (2795992 bytes)
+```
+
+Custom `.elf` files go through the **Custom** tab. Keep the page open while a
+delivery runs - the injection executes from the page. Catalog downloads need
+the PS5 online; the exploit chain itself (elfldr included) works fully
+offline thanks to the caches.
+
+## Deploying
+- **GitHub Pages:** replace the repo content with this build, commit + push,
+  open the URL on the PS5 once online (the service worker caches everything).
+- **Self-host on port 12423:** `python3 serve.py`, open
+  `http://<pc-ip>:12423/` once - the site then caches itself into PS5
+  localStorage and keeps working even if the server goes away.
 
 ## Stability notes
 Webkit may need several attempts, reload the page if the browser stalls. The kernel exploit may hang or panic the console, so reboot before trying again if that happens.
